==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|attonce}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{m|en|at}} + {{m|en|once}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ət ˈwʌns/}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-at once.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} In [[one]] group; [[together]].
#* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|Luke|14|18|cj|And they all '''atonce''' begane to make excuſe. The fyrſt ſayd vnto him: I have bought a ferme&nbsp;/ and I muſt nedes goo and ſe it&nbsp;/ I praye the have me excuſed.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|III|iv|passage=Those two great champions did '''attonce''' pursew / The fearefull damzell with incessany payns [...].}}
# [[at the same time|At the same time]]; [[simultaneously]].
#: {{ux|en|Can you pat your head and rub your belly '''at once'''?<br />He tried to eat four cookies '''at once.'''}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Lexington| title=Keeping the mighty honest: A new wave of press barons should not allow newspapers to become niche products| url=http://www.economist.com/news/united-states/21583274-new-wave-press-barons-should-not-allow-newspapers-become-niche-products-keeping| magazine={{w|The Economist}}| date=10 August 2013| volume=408| issue=8848| passage=The [''Washington''] ''Post's'' proprietor through those turbulent &#91;{{w|Watergate}}&#93; days, Katharine Graham, held a double place in Washington’s hierarchy: '''at once''' regal Georgetown hostess and scrappy newshound, ready to hold the establishment to account.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Tom Payne|title=''Sapiens: a Brief History of Humankind'' by Yuval Noah Harari, review: 'urgent questions' &#91;print version: The story of our species, 27 September 2014, p.&nbsp;R32&#93;|url=http://www.telegraph.co.uk/culture/books/bookreviews/11120947/Sapiens-a-Brief-History-of-Humankind-by-Yuval-Noah-Harari-review-urgent-questions.html| work={{w|The Daily Telegraph}} (Review)| date=26 September 2014| passage=[T]he book, constructed in short, lucid episodes, can be satisfyingly read as a sequence of provocative talks, '''at once''' well informed and vatic.}}
# [[immediately|Immediately]]; [[now]]; [[right away]].
#: {{ux|en|Tell the doctor to come '''at once'''. She is having a baby.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=4|passage=Mr. Cooke '''at once''' began a tirade against the residents of Asquith for permitting a sandy and generally disgraceful condition of the roads. So roundly did he vituperate the inn management in particular, and with such a loud flow of words, that I trembled lest he should be heard on the veranda.}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=3|passage=One saint's day in mid-term a certain newly appointed suffragan-bishop came to the school chapel, and there preached on “The Inner Life.”&nbsp; He '''at once''' secured attention by his informal method, and when presently the coughing of Jarvis {{...}} interrupted the sermon, he altogether captivated his audience with a remark about cough lozenges being cheap and easily procurable.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1960|month=March| title=Talking of Trains: The Slough derailment| journal=Trains Illustrated| page=132| text=Sparks from the derailed bogie of the train were first noticed by the signalman at Slough West box, who immediately sent to Slough Middle box the "Stop and Examine" signal, followed '''at once''' by "Obstruction Danger" when he realised that the coach was derailed.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1963|author={{w|Margery Allingham}}|title={{w|The China Governess}}| chapter=19| url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL2004261W| passage=As soon as Julia returned with a constable, Timothy, who was on the point of exhaustion, prepared to give over to him gratefully. The newcomer turned out to be a powerful youngster, fully trained and eager to help, and he stripped off his tunic '''at once'''.}}

